# Complete operator and developer documentation

## Scope and existing evidence

Document calibration, composition, one-shot LLM use, PDF, live safety, supported
hardware, profile internals, schemas, adapters, validation, and troubleshooting.

Current developer documentation now includes a verified portable-profile
container guide mapping the accepted `.atrament` design to executable manifest,
path, inventory, entry-evidence, ZIP-canonicality, rewrite, and compatibility
behavior. The guide also marks the Rust values as non-wire-schema authority and
lists the still-missing ZIP/JSON adapters, migrations, real digest computation,
resource limits, typed section schemas, golden files, and inspection command.

A verified PDF/print composition guide now maps the accepted output ADR to
executable vector authority, pre-serialization PDF page/resource intent, render
manifest inputs, preview/final invariants, print/scan evidence, and independent
visual-regression axes. It explicitly keeps PDF serialization, image/resource
adapters, manifest identity, visual comparators, and physical tolerances open.

A verified calibration-evidence guide now documents observed/inferred parameter
evidence, source/unit/confidence/correction retention, held-out separation,
resumable prompt plans, underdetermination, and downstream bounded-variation
invariants. It keeps capture geometry, extraction, minimum sample sets, quality
metrics, confidence policy, and persistence explicitly open.

A verified live physical-safety guide now documents capability disposition,
device-neutral plan intent, coordinate-calibration evidence, fail-closed dry
runs, offline traces, interruption recovery, and exact compatibility records.
It explicitly states that none of those types arm hardware or establish generic
device support. Vendor adapters, operator recovery, physical certification,
and compatibility admission/recertification policy remain open.

A verified one-shot formatting-prompt guide now documents complete initial
request inputs, prompt identity/version, explicit clipboard egress, untrusted
raw
response handling, and the separation from later command-mode refinement. It
keeps prompt serialization/identity computation, response parsing, candidate
validation, provider guidance, and end-to-end execution explicitly open.

A verified adapter-boundary guide now maps browser, localhost, clipboard, MCP,
Render, Export, and Plan contracts to their separate admission, effect, retry,
identity, persistence, and physical-safety boundaries. It also distinguishes
implemented browser/runtime behavior from contract-only CLI, MCP, file, model,
media, and hardware adapters.

A verified first-release troubleshooting guide now maps current startup,
handshake, draft, HTTP, clipboard, profile-integrity, capability, dry-run, and
recovery outcomes to fail-closed operator/developer actions. It explicitly
marks Export, CLI, MCP transport, model/media, encoders, and hardware adapters
as unavailable rather than inventing recovery procedures.

A verified documentation-coverage record now maps every category named by this
task to its current guide and marks design-only adapters, schemas, packaging,
and hardware controls unavailable until their owning implementation tasks add
real operator surfaces. This closes the documentation task for the executable
first-release surface; future implementation tasks own the documentation they
introduce.

## Finite operator quick-start and developer interface map

**Present operator quick-start:** launch the local `atrament` runtime through
its automatic browser-opening path; wait for the browser's authenticated
`Session ready` status before editing. Enter Task, Source and Model response
in their labeled editors. Use `Copy prompt` only as an explicit clipboard
action for a separately selected chat; pasting into an external provider
shares data with that provider, not with a private local model. Browser
drafts are disposable; do not assume they persist on close. Use the keyboard
skip links and visible zoom controls for preview navigation.

**Present profile utilities:** the command set available independently of
notebook editing includes `atrament profile verify INPUT.atrament`,
`check-canonical`, `inspect`, and `repack INPUT.atrament OUTPUT.atrament`
(with explicit `--replace` only for intended replacement). Back up a profile
before any replace request. Do not infer that this makes a notebook import,
PDF export, physical plot, CLI notebook API or MCP session available.

**Failure/recovery table:**

| Observed problem | Operator rule |
| --- | --- |
| Browser launch or loopback startup fails | Stop and restart via admitted automatic launcher; never print credentials |
| Handshake rejected or incompatible | Use matching local artifacts; no bypass of Host/Origin/version |
| Draft too large | Reduce the affected field; previous accepted field persists |
| Draft transport retry offered | Use visible Retry while session credential remains valid |
| Profile integrity or unsupported features | Inspect read-only; do not overwrite or guess a migration |
| No PDF/Render/Export adapter | State `Unavailable`, never fabricate a delivered file |
| Live dry-run or recovery uncertainty | Do not send motion or resume an unknown position |

**Developer interface index:** `localhost-session-runtime-contract.md`
for listener, admission and session lifecycle;
`adapter-boundary-validation.md` for browser, clipboard and unavailable
MCP/CLI boundaries; `portable-profile-container-validation.md` for manifest,
ZIP canonicalization and profile file transport;
`one-shot-formatting-prompt-validation.md` for clipboard/model request
provenance; `pdf-print-composition-validation.md` for vector/PDF design;
`handwriting-calibration-evidence-validation.md` for source evidence;
`live-physical-safety-validation.md` for Plan, dry-run and fail-closed
recovery; `first-release-troubleshooting.md` for current status meanings;
`first-release-documentation-coverage.md` for authoritative documentation
coverage. All paths are under `docs/technical/`.

**Limits:** technical Rust types are not interchangeably frozen JSON wire
schemas. The PDF/Render pipeline, notebook Import/Export, media/model
adapters, hardware transport and multiplatform installers remain designs;
the existing docs may describe their contracts but are not operational
runbooks for those missing services. No claim is made that a physical
AxiDraw or other machine is connected or supported.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** current operator controls and linked verified technical guides,
  with theoretical guidance for missing adapters, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** finite quick-start, seven-case failure table, profile CLI
  commands and interface/document index with explicit unimplemented scope.
- **Unavailable:** executable notebook/PDF exporter, vendor hardware runbook,
  release installer manual and full future adapter API documentation.
